Design for Df Refurbishing is an industrial process where items are inspected and repaired to "functional condition". Through a variety of industrial processes, only to those parts of the product that have failed or are badly worn of are disassembled and then reassembled with both used or new parts.  
The product should be adequately designed accordingly with the geographical area, workforce knowledge and refurbishment capacity where it is going to be refurbished. The refurbished product should meet applicable technical standards or regulatory requirements to be used for the purposed that was originally intended. In Design for Refurbishment, Design for Disassembly strategy plays a key role and it is related mainly with cost and time of disassembly and reassembly of the product.   
Company and retailers often use terms "refurbished" and "remanufactured" interchangeably, although that is not correct. The key difference is that with a refurbished product, unlike a remanufactured product, the manufacturer does not provide a product warranty because it is not restored to "like-new condition".